course: everyday
lesson: 273
title: Shops, restaurants and libraries
level: primary-4
stars: 1
explain: In shops and other shared places there are a few simple rules: wait your turn, be honest with money, keep your card PIN secret, and speak quietly where others are reading.
explain: If you get lost in a shop or mall, you stay where you are or go to a till or checkout and tell a staff member, who can help find your family.
explain: Some rules differ by country: in the USA the price on the shelf often does not include sales tax, which is added at the checkout, and people usually leave a tip of about 15 to 20 percent in a restaurant.
explain: A boy in Chicago is given a dollar too much in change; he says, "I think you gave me too much," and the cashier thanks him.
fact: If you get lost in a shop or mall, you stay where you are or go to a till or checkout and tell a staff member.
fact: If you get too much change, you tell the cashier and give it back, because that is honest.
fact: When you pay with cash, you count your money first and check your change.
fact: When you pay by card, you keep the PIN secret and cover the keypad, because others could watch and steal it.
fact: You wait your turn in a queue or line at the checkout, because pushing in is rude.
fact: In the USA the shelf price may not include sales tax, which is added at the checkout.
fact: On escalators in malls you hold the rail and never run up the down escalator.
fact: In US restaurants people usually leave a tip of about 15 to 20 percent, because it is part of the waiter's pay.
fact: In the library you speak quietly, because others are reading and studying.
fact: A late library book is returned as soon as possible, because some libraries charge fines.
fact: A damaged library book is shown to the librarian, who can fix or replace it.
exercise: You lose your mum in a big shopping mall. What do you do? | Stay where you are or tell a staff member at a till | Leave the mall and walk home the way you came | Go with a stranger who offers help | Search every shop by yourself until you find her
exercise: Why is a staff member a good person to ask when you are lost in a shop? | Staff can help find your family | Staff will give you a free toy | Staff know everyone's mum | Staff have to drive you home
exercise: The cashier gives you too much change. What do you do? | Tell the cashier and give it back | Keep it, because it was their mistake | Spend it quickly on sweets before leaving | Give it to the next person in line
exercise: Why should you count your money before paying with cash? | It helps avoid mistakes with change | It makes the cashier work faster | Cashiers ask everyone to count aloud | It keeps the coins clean
exercise: You pay with your mum's card while she watches. What do you do with the PIN? | Cover the keypad and keep it secret | Say it out loud so the cashier knows | Write it on the back of the card | Show it to the person behind you
exercise: Why should you cover the keypad when typing a PIN? | Others could watch and use it to steal | The keypad works better when it is covered | The card works better when it is covered | It keeps the keypad warm and dry in the rain
exercise: There is a long line at the checkout. What do you do? | Wait your turn at the end of the line | Go to the front because you have one thing | Push in next to a friend | Leave your basket and go
exercise: A toy in a US shop says $10.00. Why might you pay more at the till? | Sales tax is added at the checkout | The till always rounds up | The cashier adds a fee for children | Shelf prices are a guess
exercise: How should you ride a mall escalator? | Hold the rail and never run up the down escalator | Run up the down escalator for fun | Sit on the steps to slide down | Lean over the rail to wave at people on the floor below
exercise: Your family eats in a restaurant in Boston. What is usual when paying? | Leave a tip of about 15 to 20 percent | Pay only for the drinks | Leave no tip, because tipping is rude in America | Leave a tip of half the bill
exercise: Why do people tip waiters in the USA? | It is part of the waiter's pay | It pays for the cook's ingredients | It is a fine for staying too long at the table | It buys a free dessert next time
exercise: You want to tell your friend a joke in the library. What do you do? | Speak quietly or wait until you are outside | Say it loudly so everyone can laugh along with you | Shout from across the room | Tell it during story time
exercise: Your library book is two weeks late. What do you do? | Return it as soon as possible | Keep it, because it is too late now | Hide it on a library shelf | Throw it away so nobody knows
exercise: Your little brother tore a page in a library book. What do you do? | Show the librarian, who can fix or replace it | Put it back quietly on the shelf | Tape it and say nothing to anyone at the library | Take the page out and keep it
test: A girl in a supermarket in Cork notices the cashier gave her 5 euros too much. What is honest? | Point it out and hand it back | Keep it quietly for her savings | Give it to charity without saying anything | Wait to see if the cashier notices
test: A boy is lost in a big department store and a man in the car park offers to take him to his mum. What should he do? | Stay inside and tell a staff member at a till | Go with the man to the car park | Run outside to look for the car | Hide until the shop closes, then look for his mum outside
test: A man stands close behind you while you type your card PIN. What do you do? | Cover the keypad with your other hand | Type faster so he cannot see | Tell him the PIN so he stops looking | Let him type it for you
test: A sign in a shop in Texas says "Pens $2". A girl has exactly $2. What might happen? | She might not have enough, as sales tax is added | She will get change back | The pen will be free | The price will drop at the till because she is a child buying alone
test: Why would a waiter in New York be unhappy if a family left no tip? | Tips are part of the waiter's pay there | The food would have to be paid for again | Waiters get paid only by the cook in the kitchen | The family has broken a law and will be fined by the police
test: Which is true about the library? | People speak quietly because others are reading | The library is a good place to play tag | Late books never matter, so keep them as long as you like | Damaged books should be hidden
test: A boy pushes in front of an old lady in the checkout line. Why is this wrong? | Everyone waits their turn; pushing in is rude | Old people are always slower | The cashier only serves boys | The lady had more shopping, so she should have let him go first
test: A girl wants to run up the down escalator in the mall for fun. What could happen? | She could fall and knock into others | The escalator will go the other way | She will get there faster | Nothing, because escalators are slow
test: A boy finds a library book under his bed, months late. What should he do? | Return it as soon as possible and tell the librarian | Keep it forever now | Post it back without a name | Leave it outside the door at night so nobody sees who brought it
test: lost my mum in the mall!! | Stay put or tell a staff member at a till | Go outside to look in the car park | Ask any stranger to drive you home | Leave the mall and walk home
test: man behind me at the card machine is watching | Cover the keypad when you type your PIN | Say the PIN out loud | Type it slowly so he sees | Give him the card so he can stop looking over your shoulder
test: in the us the sign said $10 but they want more at the till?? | Sales tax is added at the checkout | The shop is cheating you | The price went up in a minute | They charged you twice
test: queue's long, can i just push in at the front | No, wait your turn | Yes, if you're quick | Yes, kids always go first in a queue | Only if you say sorry after
explain: A five-year-old in a big mall in Ohio wandered off to look at toys and went out of the shop to find his mum; it took security an hour to find him, and his mum was terrified.
explain: A girl in Sydney let a stranger stand close behind her at a cash machine; he saw her mum's PIN and later money was taken from the account.
fact: A lost child is found fastest when they stay inside the shop or go to a till, because that is where staff and parents look first.
fact: Someone who sees a PIN and gets the card can take money, and getting it back can be slow or impossible.
test: lost mum in the shopping centre!! go outside n look? | No, stay in the shop and ask someone at the till | Yes, she is probably in the car park | Yes, walk home by yourself | Yes, follow anyone who offers to help you find her
test: guy behind me at the cash machine is reading over my shoulder | Cover the keypad and tell your adult | Type faster so he cannot see | Let him see, a PIN is not secret | Ask him to hold the card for you while you type it in
test: escalator going down, my cousin wants to run up it for fun | No, he could fall and get hurt or trap fingers | Yes, it's good exercise | Yes, if nobody is on it | Yes, if he holds the rail tight with both hands on the way up
test: heard a little kid got lost in the mall and "ran out to the car park and nearly got run over", my nan said. lesson? | If lost, stay inside or go to a till and tell staff | Run outside to look for your parents' car | Hide somewhere till the shop closes | Ask any adult to drive you home
test: my mate says some lady watched a man's PIN and "emptied his whole bank, like millions". what's prob exaggerated | The millions; the real lesson is cover the keypad | That people can watch a PIN | That a PIN should be secret | That cards can need a PIN at the till
test: kids are saying the waiter at the pizza place is "stupid" cos he got their order wrong. pass it on? | No, don't spread unkind gossip about him | Yes, everyone should know about him | Yes, and give him a bad name online with a review | Yes, so nobody sits at his tables
exam: Which of the following statements most accurately characterises the conduct required of a child who becomes separated from an adult within a shop? | The child shall remain inside or go to a till and inform staff | The child shall exit the premises to search the car park | The child shall conceal themselves until closing time | The child shall accept a lift from any willing adult
exam: Whereas a cashier has tendered change in excess of the sum due, identify the course of action consistent with honesty. | The excess shall be declared and returned to the cashier | The excess may be retained as the cashier's own error | The excess shall be placed in a charity box | The excess shall be spent before leaving the premises of the shop
exam: In respect of restaurants in the United States, state the customary gratuity. | Approximately fifteen to twenty per cent of the bill | Approximately one to two per cent of the bill | Approximately one half of the bill | No gratuity whatsoever is customary in that country
exam: Identify the principal reason for which conversation within a library is conducted quietly. | Other persons are engaged in reading and study | Librarians impose fines for every spoken word | Books are damaged by loud noise | Speech is prohibited by national statute in all public libraries
